From 0ec553a7028f6e4463b439fe9d03ec6d240a7712 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 1aeo <github@1aeo.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 00:30:57 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] relay_metrics: Count tunneled directory streams closed unread
 by cause

tor_relay_begindir_request_total counts the tunneled directory streams
an HSDir closes without reading a request, as "no_request" (nothing
arrived) and "circuit_dead" (a request dropped unread because its stream
or circuit was closing), but not what closed them. By the time we count
them, the cause is gone: connection_edge_destroy() rewrites the stream's
end reason to DESTROY, and the exit connection is freed before the
directory connection reads its end.

Note the first cause where it happens. A RELAY_END on the stream, a DATA
cell over its window, a stall, and the out-of-memory handler closing the
stream go on the directory connection. The BEGIN_DIR rate limit, the
onion service request defenses, a full cell queue and the out-of-memory
handler go on the circuit, since the reasons they close it with don't
say which closed it. When we mark an OR circuit for close, we tell the
directory connections of its streams why, and otherwise map DESTROY, a
closed channel, a protocol violation and any other reason. A stream we
are closing already keeps its own cause, or none.

relay_note_dir_request() counts each of these requests once, by that
cause, as tor_relay_begindir_close_reason_total{type,prev_hop,reason}:
the reasons of each type and previous hop add up to its requests.

The causes take four bits of padding on the directory connection and on
the circuit: neither grows.

Part of #41339.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
